Oxidation and Reduction of Cu in the Triangular Building Blocks. Interestingly, all the FDM-4–7 crystals are colorless when they were firstly crystallized in capped vials (Figure 5a), indicating that \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) ions from cupric nitrate were reduced during the synthesis, and the Cu in these as-synthesized MOF structures is monovalent. A close re-examination of the previously reported FDM-3 recorded the same observation. Reduction of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) to \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{I})\) is not rare in MOF synthesis, and possibly assisted by the solvents as the reductant.9f,i,n However, to our surprise, after loosening the vial caps, the crystal color transformed to light blue immediately for all five MOFs, suggesting that some \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{I})\) in the framework were oxidized to \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) upon contacting with ambient air (Figure 5b). In view of this, we performed the FDM-6 synthesis in a sealed tube filled with \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). The formed FDM-6 crystals keep colorless for weeks until the tube is opened, indicating that the \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{I})\) in the MOF structure is stable until it contacts with air.

The fast oxidation addresses a challenge in the accurate structure determination of the Cu-based SBUs in these MOFs by SXRD. Depending on the length of air contact time before and during data collection, partial or full oxidation of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{I})\) occurs, and therefore different SBUs (3a or 3b in Figure 1) were presented. In the reported structures from SXRD, FDM-4 and FDM-5 feature the 3a units in the frameworks; on the other hand, FDM-6 and FDM-7 has the 3b as the building blocks, in which the square-planar coordination geometry of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) is fulfilled by \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)as the terminal ligand. However, considering all the pristine crystals being colorless, we propose that the as-synthesized FDM3–7 have the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}_{3}(\mathrm{PyO})_{3}\) units as the building blocks, and
