Based on the single crystal structures, CHN elemental analysis, and ICP-AES (see SI for details), we conclude formulas of all the as-synthesized MOFs based on \(\mathrm{Cu}^{13}(\mathrm{PyC})_{3},\) fully-oxidized MOFs based on \(\mathrm{Cu^{II}}_{3}(\mu{-}\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{PyC})_{3},\) and mixed-charge MOFs based on \(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3-\mathrm{x}}(\mu{-}\mathrm{OH})_{\mathrm{x}}\) - \(\mathrm{x}_{/3}(\mathrm{PyC})_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{3-\mathrm{x}}\) \(\mathbf{\dot{x}}=\mathbf{0}{-}3)\) in Table S1. For example, the formula of the as-synthesized FDM-4 is \(\{(\mathrm{Zn}_{3}\mathrm{O})_{5}[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{PyC})_{6}](\mathrm{PyC})_{6}]\cdot[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]_{6}\) . When FDM-4 is fully oxidized, the formula changes to \(\{(\mathrm{Zn}_{3}\mathrm{O})_{5}[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mu-\)

\(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{PyC})_{3}[\mathrm{PyC})_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{18}]\cdot[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{16}\) . It is noteworthy that one OH- from the isolated 3n-based complex which stays in the pores to compensate the charge but could not be located by SXRD, is substituted with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to keep the MOF structure neutral upon oxidation.

X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a powerful tool to examine the valence of Cu in FDM-3–7, and it shows mixed charge for all five MOFs even with minimum exposure to the air during the MOF drying process (wash with N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), solvent exchange with \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) , followed by vacuum dry at room temperature). Specifically, as shown in Figure 5c, an intense asymmetrical Cu \({\bf2p}_{3/2}\) photoelectron peak along with satellite peaks indicate mixed valence state of Cu in FDM-6. This peak can be deconvoluted into two contributions located at 932.9

and \(934.4\,\mathrm{eV}\) , corresponding to \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2},\) respectively. Integration of the \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peak, and \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peak with \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) satellite peaks (941.4 and \(944.3\;\mathrm{eV})\) ) gives the calculated ratio of \(\mathrm{Cu(I):Cu(II)}\) being \({\sim}2.01.0\) in FDM-6. Other investigated MOFs also show mixed valence state of Cu, with the ratios of \(\mathrm{Cu(I):Cu(II)}\) in FDM-3, -4, -5, and \({\sim}7\) being \~1.11.9, 2.20.8, 1.0:2.0, and 1.3:1.7, respectively (Figure Sn, S13, S15, and S17). These results suggest that FDM-3–7, featuring the same Cu-based SBUs, but with completely different spatial arrangement of SBUs and distinct local electronic structures around these SBUs, show varied oxidation rate even the same sample drying procedure with similar air exposure time was applied. Interestingly, complete oxidation of these MOFs within a short period could be achieved by adding additional oxidants, as indicated by the appearance of only \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peaks \((933.4\;\mathrm{eV}\) in Figure S19, 933.6 eV in Figure S20, and 933.7 eV in Figure S21) in the cases of FDM-4 and FDM-6 oxidized with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},\) and FDM-3 oxidized with \(\mathrm{Ag^{+}}\) , respectively.

Most of the reports on MOFs with Cu-based triangular SBUs suggest the oxidation state of Cu being either \(^{2+}\) or \(^{1+}\) , with \(^{2+}\) dominant in the literatures. Few structures made from bipyrazole show very slow oxidation of \({\mathrm{Cu}}(1)\) in the air, as indicated by the mild color change of the crystals.90,12 However, FDM-3–7 are the first MOF examples where the oxidation of \({\mathrm{Cu}}(1)\) happens rapidly and can be
