![]('img_url')

Oxidation of Carbon Monoxide by FDM-3–7. The study of carbon monoxide oxidation over Cu-based catalysts has its significance in the exhaust emission control.15 \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) is known to catalyze the oxidation of CO by the use of valence variation to seize or release oxygen readily.16 With all the \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites accessible in the activated FDM-3– 7, we investigated their catalytic activities in CO oxidation using a fix bed flow reactor. CO ( \(1\%\) in air) was passed through the bed after the MOFs were pretreated in He at \(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{2\,\mathrm{h}}\) , and the effluent gases were analyzed by GC. Figure 6a shows that the activated FDM- \(\cdot3{-}7\) can catalyze the oxidation of CO, with complete conversion to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at \~210 \(^{\circ}C\) for FDM-4, and \({\sim}220~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for FDM-3, -6, and -7. The PXRD of these four MOFs after CO oxidation at up to \(240~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) showed that all the peaks corresponded to the pristine MOF structures (Figure S23, S24, S26, and S27), suggesting the MOFs retain their structures during the reactions. Furthermore, no peak from either \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}\) or \(\mathrm{CuO}\) was observed, confirming the catalytic active sites are the triangular Cu-based modules which are accessible to CO guests. In the case of the activated FDM-5, full conversion was not achieved, probably due to the relative instability of the bimetallic \(Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}\) (SBU 3c) and \(Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}(\mathrm{NN})\) (SBU 5c) under high temperature, as evidenced by the early weight loss in TGA (Figure S22) and peak disappearance in PXRD after CO oxidation (Figure S25).

The temperature required for \(50\%\) CO conversion \((T_{1/2})\) is 200, 180, 215, 195, and 190 \(^{\circ}C\) for FDM-3–7, respectively. Our control studies on \(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}\) based HKUST-17 activated by heating at 200 \(^{\circ}C\) under vacuum shows no activity at \(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Although HKUST-1 activated with higher temperature (230 and \(250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) ) have shown CO oxidation activities below \(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) previously,18 it is unclear if the activity is from the reduced \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) in the framework90 or \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}\) from the decomposition of HKUST-1,14b,20 Overall, compared to HKUST-1 and a reported copper pyrazolatecarboxylate \(\mathrm{MOF}^{21}\) with redox-inactive \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) -b-based units under similar activation methods, lower temperature was recorded for \(100\%\) CO conversion using the MOFs presented. We conclude that the elevated performance is due to the high density of \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites in the framework, and their aptness in valence variation during the conversion. In addition, high surface areas and large pore openings of mesoporous FDM-3–7 guarantee the accessibility of active sites, and free diffusion of CO, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) .

The interaction between CO and Cu sites in FDM-3–7 were further studied by in situ diffuse reflectance infrared

Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS). Figure 6b shows the DRIFTS of the FDM-7 after three stepwise treatments (preheated at \(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) under He, exposed to \(5\,\mathrm{v.\%}\) CO flow for 30 mins, and purged with He for 15 min after CO exposure), and the DRIFTS for other MOFs are provided in Figure S28-S31. After exposing FDM-7 to CO, two peaks at 2171 and \(2109~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) were observed. Upon purging with He, the peak at 2171 \(\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) disappears, and the peak at 2109 \(\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) shifts slightly to \(2105~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . It is known that CO adsorption on \(\mathrm{{Cu^{2+}}}\) or \(\mathrm{{Cu^{0}}}\) is very weak and could be easily removed by purging at room temperature, thus the remaining peak at \(2105\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be assigned to the stretching vibration of the typical \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}\) bond after CO chemisorption on \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites88a,20,22. For FDM-3–6, similar DRIFTS results were obtained, confirming the strong interaction between the CO and \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites in the MOFs, and the subsequent CO activation.

\(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\) Decomposition Catalyzed by FDM-3–7. The interchangeability of Cu valences in FDM-3–7 drives us to further evaluate their catalytic performances in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) decomposition. Transition metal catalyzed decomposition of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) usually involves redox-active species, such as \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})/{\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites in Cu-amine complex or \(\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}\) sites in iron oxide.33 When \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) ( \(30\%\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) \(1.0\ \mathrm{mL}\) ) was added to the activated FDM-3 ( \(\left\langle{\sim}0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}\right.\) Cu sites) in DMF, violent \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) bubbles emerge from the surface of the MOF crystals immediately and the gas evolving process continues for up to 24 hours (Video S1 in the SI). FDM4–7 show similar catalytic activities in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) decomposition with activated FDM-3, confirming the decomposition is catalyzed by the same Cu-based triangular building blocks. In contrast, experiment performed on activated HKUST-1 shows no \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) released from the crystals (Video S2 in the SI), indicating that the decomposition of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) cannot be catalyzed by Lewis acidic \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) sites as in HKUST-1, but by the redox-active \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})/{\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites as in FDM-3–7. It is worth noting that the crystals color turn darker at the initial \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) decomposition stage, indicating some \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) were possibly oxidized to \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) by the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) released.

With the help of stirring, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) decomposition rate can be accelerated to less than 6 hours for the aforementioned reaction, with \(\sim100\%\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) conversion observed by the time interval measurement of the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) volume (Figure 6c). We picked the data from 50 to 100 min after \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) contacted with the five MOFs, to look at the kinetics of the reaction, for the reason that (i) during this period the effect of diffusion barrier of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into the MOF pores can be considered as constant, and (ii) a good amount of released \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) keeps the relative error of the measured \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) volume small. As shown in Figure S32, pseudo-first order kinetic patterns were observed for all the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) decomposition catalyzed by five different MOFs. The \(k_{\mathrm{obs}}\) of the reactions are \((1.64\,\pm\) \(0.07)\times10^{-2}\) , \((2.00\pm0.06)\times10^{-2}\) , \((0.66\pm0.02)\times10^{-2}\) , \((1.24\,\pm\) \(0.04)\times10^{-2}\) , and \((1.77\pm0.07)\times10^{-2}\,\mathrm{min^{-1}}\) for FDM-3–7, respectively. Furthermore, the systems still retain their activities for several batches without any regeneration process, as indicated by the full \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) evolution upon injecting \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in equal amounts every \(6~\mathrm{{h}}\) for FDM-4 (Figure 6d). After five sequential batches, assuming all Cu sites in FDM-4 are
active, each Cu site involves at least 500 cycles of rigorous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), conversion without obvious decrease in the reaction rate. These MOFs lost their high crystallinity after \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) decomposition experiment according to PXRD (Figure S33), indicating possible structural amorphisation due to the frequent Cu valence interchange and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) coordination competition. However, the crystals remain their original shape after the catalysis (without stirring, Figure S34), suggesting the frameworks still hold their integrity. What is more, the reaction solution remains colorless and no precipitate forms upon addition of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) into the supernatant, indicating no Cu from the framework is leached into the solution.

Pristine FDM-3–7 as Benzyl Alcohol Oxidation Catalysts. Complexes and MOFs based on Cu have been shown to catalyze the aerobic oxidation of benzyl alcohol.24 One of the proposed mechanisms involving \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) site includes its oxidation to \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), followed by the alcohol's oxidation to aldehyde by the \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) species and co-catalyst.24a The redox facility and accessibility of the \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites is the key in achieving efficient catalysis. However, MOFs containing accessible and redox-active \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites for alcohol oxidation are in scarce, with only one example of introducing \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites by post-synthetic modification.24b With \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{I})\) sites decorating on the pore surfaces and taking up more than \(19\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) in the solids, FDM-3–7 were examined as the catalysts for the aerobic oxidation of benzyl alcohol in the open air at \(70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) by using 2,2,6,6-tetramethyl-1-piperidinyloxy (TEMPO) and \(N\)-methylimidazole (NMI) as co-catalysts, and the yields were checked by \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR. As shown in Table 2, without MOF catalyst, the reaction shows very low yield \((2\%)\); however, all the five pristine MOFs investigated show remarkable catalytic activities in its oxidation to benzaldehyde. Specifically, the yields of benzaldehyde are \(98\%\), \(>99\%\), \(62\%\), \(>99\%\), and \(98\%\) for the reactions catalyzed by activated FDM-3–7 in \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The unsatisfactory yield in the case of FDM-5 is due to the smaller pore opening (with the aperture diagonal of \(6.6\,\mathrm{\AA}\)) and possible lower chemical stability. Indeed, a high yield of \(70\%\), \(83\%\), \(84\%\) and \(87\%\) were achieved for FDM-3, -4, -6 and -7 as catalysts within the first 12 h. The pores inside these MOFs allow free movement of benzyl alcohol in the crystals and close contact with the active Cu sites without any steric hindrance.

Pristine FDM-3–7 show great activities in the alcohol oxidation, although our preliminary studies on the PXRD reveal the solids lost their crystallinities after catalytic reactions. We understand that the participation of several Zn-carboxylate based SBUs (such as 3c and 6a) in these MOFs may raise chemical stability issues when they were applied as catalysts in the reactions with TEMPO and NIM. To elucidate the heterogeneous nature of the porous catalysts, we performed their substrate size selectivity studies under the same condition. 3,4,5-Trimethoxybenzyl alcohol (TMBA) was picked for its larger size \((\sim\!9.5\times9.2\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) than benzyl alcohol \((\sim\!6.0\times4.3\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\). Based on the single crystal structures, FDM-3, -4, -6, and -7 have their pore openings all at \(\sim\!11.0\) Å, which restrain the free movement of TMBA inside the crystals to some extent. Consequently, drastic drop in the yields \((13\%\) for FDM-4 and \(15\%\) for FDM-6 in \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), Table 2)

was observed. These results suggest that the oxidation of benzyl alcohol mainly happens inside the MOF crystals rather than in homogeneous solutions; and the size and shape of TMBA molecules have made them (i) diffuse very slowly in the pores, and (ii) difficult to contact with the Cu active sites due to space restriction. Overall, the size and shape selectivity towards the substrates confirms the heterogeneous nature of the MOF catalysts, whose structural character still stands and pore accessibility still remains. With MOF building blocks for higher chemical stability available,25 researches on new structures incorporating these accessible redox-active sites will provide materials with unparalleled catalytic performances.

